A bundle carrying development dependencies is a bigger bundle, and the copy
that assembles it passes no timeout of its own — so it takes Laravel's default
sixty seconds, which a debug build exceeds. Sixty seconds is a budget rather
than a correctness property, and what it produces when it runs out is an rsync
killed halfway: a partial tree, a build that fails somewhere later, and nothing
saying the copy is what ended.

--- a/vendor/nativephp/mobile/src/Support/BundleFileManager.php
+++ b/vendor/nativephp/mobile/src/Support/BundleFileManager.php
@@ -80 +80 @@
-        $result = Process::run("rsync -a --copy-links {$excludeFlags} \"{$source}/\" \"{$destination}/\"");
+        $result = Process::timeout(600)->run("rsync -a --copy-links {$excludeFlags} \"{$source}/\" \"{$destination}/\"");
